var endpointValidateCmd = &cobra.Command{
	Annotations: map[string]string{reference.Result: "The command prints:\n\n```ansi\n$ osapi dsl endpoint validate\n\x1b[1;38;2;0;213;255mValidation:\x1b[0m\n  \x1b[38;2;10;143;176mGroups:\x1b[0m  10\n  \x1b[38;2;10;143;176mValid:\x1b[0m   yes\n```\n\nWith `--json`, the command prints:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"groups\": 10,\n  \"valid\": true\n}\n```"},
	Args:        cobra.NoArgs,
	Example:     "  osapi dsl endpoint validate\n  osapi dsl endpoint validate --json",
	Long:        "It prints each problem with its group, method, rule and message, then the\nfix of each rule, and exits 1 when a rule fails. `osapi dsl endpoint\nspec get` describes each part of a group.\n\nThe rules:\n\n- `secret`: No secret.Value input field is tagged in:\"path\" or in:\"query\", or telemetry:\"attr\": a URL goes in logs, and a span is no place for a secret. Fix: Send the secret in the body: drop its in tag, or tag it in:\"body\" or in:\"form\". Remove telemetry:\"attr\" from it.\n- `example`: Each flag that an Example names is a flag of the method's command: a field of its input, a field of the struct that in:\"body\" names, or --json, --debug or --osapi-file. Fix: Spell each flag of the example as the command has it: osapi dsl endpoint get --name <group> lists the methods, and the command's --help lists its flags.\n- `prints`: The output that Prints gives an example is a value of the method's output type, and Prints follows an Example. Fix: Give Prints a value of the method's output type, after the Example that it shows.\n- `in`: Each in tag of an input field is path, query, form or body. Fix: Correct the value. A field with another value is read from the request body, not from where the tag says.\n- `reason`: Each error that a method maps has a reason of endpoint.Reasons. Fix: Use a Reason constant, such as endpoint.ReasonNotManaged, in Because.\n- `field`: The field that Location names is a json field of the method's output, and the field that Example[T] names is a json field of T. Fix: Correct the name to the json name of the field, such as job_id.\n- `area`: The group has an area, one of area.All, so the UI, the SDK's pages and the docs site can place its operations. Fix: Add endpoint.Area(...) to the group's definition with a constant of package area, such as area.Fleet. Add a new area to area.All with its label first.\n- `name`: The group and each method have a name of lowercase letters and digits, starting with a letter. Fix: Name the group as its path segment, such as agent, and each method for what it does, such as list or drain.\n- `methods`: The group has at least one method. Fix: Add a method with endpoint.Get, Post, Put or Delete.\n- `unique`: No two methods of a group have one name. Fix: Give each method its own name.\n- `segment`: Each Sub and Action segment is lowercase letters and digits. Fix: Use one word, such as pending or drain.\n- `action`: A method with an Action has a key: an input field tagged in:\"path\". Fix: Tag the field the action is on in:\"path\", or use Sub for a fixed path.\n- `key`: An input has at most one field tagged in:\"path\". Fix: Tag one field in:\"path\"; a group has one key.\n- `id`: Each operationId is used once, and starts with a capital letter, as each operationId of the API does. Fix: Give the method its own ID with .ID, in Pascal case, such as GetAgents.\n- `route`: No two methods have one verb and path. Fix: Change the verb, the Sub or the Action.\n- `handler`: A group that Uses a type gives each method a handler, func(context.Context, D, In) (Out, error), with D that type and In and Out the method's types. A group with a handler Uses a type. Fix: Add .Handle(fn) to each method, with the signature that the message names, and endpoint.Uses[Deps]() to Define.\n- `subdoc`: Each Sub segment that makes a command of its own, such as stale in osapi client file stale list, has a SubDoc: the one line of help of that command. Fix: Add endpoint.SubDoc(\"segment\", \"...\") to Define, with what the segment's commands do, such as Read a trace from the tracing backend.\n- `validate`: Each input field has a validate tag: its rules, or omitempty for an optional field with none. The spec carries the whole tag, and the server answers 400 to a value that breaks it. Fix: Add validate:\"...\" to the field, such as validate:\"required,min=1,max=255\", or validate:\"omitempty\" for an optional field with no other rule.\n- `fails`: Each rule of an input field has a .Fails input that breaks it, which the API refuses with 400. An endpoint's required needs one, with the field empty; a behavior's needs none, as its generated tests leave the field out. A path field's required, and a min of 1 on a string that is required, optional or in the path, need none: their only bad value is empty. Fix: Add .Fails(\"<what is wrong>\", <Input>{...}) to the method, with the other fields valid and the field's value breaking the rule that the message names, such as a name of 256 characters for max=255.\n- `optional`: An optional field of a write's request body, with omitempty in its validate tag, has omitempty in its json tag too, so a client leaves it out when it is empty instead of sending \"\" or 0, which its other rules can refuse. Fix: Add ,omitempty to the field's json tag, such as json:\"reason,omitempty\".\n- `schema`: The spec builds: each input and output type is one that JSON can encode, and each validate rule has a schema. Fix: Use structs, slices, maps, strings, numbers, bools and time.Time; not channels or functions. Register the schema of a custom validate rule with rule.Register, beside its validator in internal/validation.\n",
	RunE: func(cmd *cobra.Command, _ []string) error {
		return clicommand.Run(cmd, endpointdsl.Group, "validate", endpointdsl.ValidateInput{})
	},
	Short: "Check every endpoint group against the rules of a definition",
	Use:   "validate",
}

